WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.660
Some designs have legitimate shared
ownership.

2
00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.320
A configuration object may be used by
several components.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.665
A graph node may be reached through
multiple paths.

4
00:00:07.745 --> 00:00:09.694
A service client may be shared by many
tasks.

5
00:00:09.774 --> 00:00:12.934
Rust supports these patterns with
reference-counted smart pointers,

6
00:00:13.014 --> 00:00:14.489
primarily `Rc<T>` and `Arc<T>`.

7
00:00:14.569 --> 00:00:17.203
`Rc<T>` provides shared ownership inside a
single thread.

8
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:20.812
It keeps a strong reference count and
drops the inner value when the final strong

9
00:00:20.892 --> 00:00:21.629
owner goes away.

10
00:00:21.709 --> 00:00:25.501
It is useful when several parts of a
single-threaded structure need ownership-level

11
00:00:25.581 --> 00:00:28.162
access to the same value without copying
the value itself.

12
00:00:28.242 --> 00:00:31.929
`Arc<T>` provides atomically
reference-counted shared ownership that can cross

13
00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:32.905
thread boundaries.

14
00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:34.881
It is common in threaded and async
systems,

15
00:00:34.961 --> 00:00:38.384
especially for shared configuration,
clients, caches, pools, and handles.

16
00:00:38.464 --> 00:00:39.676
The atomic count has a cost,

17
00:00:39.756 --> 00:00:43.232
so `Arc<T>` should be chosen because
thread-safe shared ownership is needed,

18
00:00:43.312 --> 00:00:45.682
not as a default replacement for ordinary
ownership.

19
00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:47.817
Shared ownership is not duplicate
ownership.

20
00:00:47.897 --> 00:00:51.478
The pointer coordinates a single
underlying value and a single eventual cleanup

21
00:00:51.558 --> 00:00:51.874
event.

22
00:00:51.954 --> 00:00:55.589
That distinction matters because cleanup
timing becomes distributed across all

23
00:00:55.669 --> 00:00:56.353
strong owners.

24
00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:59.752
A value may live longer than any one
caller expects if another owner remains

25
00:00:59.832 --> 00:01:01.148
somewhere else in the system.

26
00:01:01.228 --> 00:01:03.282
Shared ownership is also not shared
mutation.

27
00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:06.049
`Rc<T>` and `Arc<T>` let multiple owners
keep a value alive;

28
00:01:06.129 --> 00:01:08.604
they do not by themselves make mutation
safe or allowed.

29
00:01:08.684 --> 00:01:09.948
If shared state must change,

30
00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:13.136
the design needs an appropriate pattern
such as interior mutability,

31
00:01:13.216 --> 00:01:16.376
synchronization, message passing, or a
narrower ownership boundary.

32
00:01:16.456 --> 00:01:19.616
During review, ask why shared ownership is
needed, who creates owners,

33
00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:21.803
where owners are stored, when they are
dropped,

34
00:01:21.883 --> 00:01:23.937
whether any owner can live unexpectedly
long,

35
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.177
and whether mutation is controlled
separately.

36
00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:27.732
Reference counting is powerful,

37
00:01:27.812 --> 00:01:30.340
but lifecycle clarity must be maintained
deliberately.
